### What are Flink windows?

Windows in Flink group an unbounded stream into finite buckets. There are two broad families: time-based windows (event time or processing time) and count-based windows (fixed number of elements). The four shapes are tumbling, sliding, session, and global.

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.assigners.*;
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.windowing.time.Time;

// Tumbling: 1-minute non-overlapping windows
stream.keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    .sum("amount");

// Sliding: 1-minute window, 10-second slide
stream.keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .window(SlidingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1), Time.seconds(10)))
    .sum("amount");

// Session: closes after 5-minute gap
stream.keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .window(EventTimeSessionWindows.withGap(Time.minutes(5)))
    .sum("amount");

// Global: one window, custom trigger
stream.keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .window(GlobalWindows.create())
    .trigger(CountTrigger.of(100))
    .sum("amount");
```

### How do tumbling windows work?

A tumbling window of size S produces non-overlapping slots. Each event belongs to exactly one window, determined by its event time divided by S. This is the most common window type for periodic aggregations.

```java
// Per-account transaction count every 1 minute
DataStream<Tuple2<String, Long>> counts = transactions
    .keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    .apply(new WindowFunction<Transaction, Tuple2<String, Long>,
            String, TimeWindow>() {
        @Override
        public void apply(String key, TimeWindow window,
                Iterable<Transaction> input,
                Collector<Tuple2<String, Long>> out) {
            long count = 0;
            for (Transaction t : input) count++;
            out.collect(Tuple2.of(key, count));
        }
    });
```

```text
Time:    |--- 10:00-10:01 ---|--- 10:01-10:02 ---|--- 10:02-10:03 ---|
Events:  | T1  T2  T3  T4    | T5  T6             | T7  T8  T9       |
Count:   | 4                 | 2                  | 3                |
```

### How do sliding windows work?

A sliding window has a size and a slide. A one-minute window sliding every ten seconds emits a view every ten seconds, and each event can belong to multiple windows. This gives a smoother, more frequent view at the cost of more computation.

```java
// 1-minute window, sliding every 10 seconds
DataStream<Tuple2<String, BigDecimal>> sliding = transactions
    .keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .window(SlidingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1), Time.seconds(10)))
    .reduce((a, b) -> new Transaction(
        a.getAccountId(), a.getAmount().add(b.getAmount())));

// Each event lands in size/slide = 60/10 = 6 windows
```

### What is a watermark?

A watermark is a special marker that flows with the stream and asserts that event time has progressed to a certain point. It is computed as the maximum event time seen so far minus an out-of-orderness tolerance. When the watermark crosses a window's end time, the window fires.

```java
import org.apache.flink.api.common.eventtime.WatermarkStrategy;
import java.time.Duration;

// Bounded out-of-orderness: watermark = max_seen - 5 seconds
DataStream<Transaction> withWatermarks = transactions
    .assignTimestampsAndWatermarks(
        WatermarkStrategy.<Transaction>forBoundedOutOfOrderness(
                Duration.ofSeconds(5))
            .withTimestampAssigner(
                (txn, ts) -> txn.getEventTimeMillis())
    );
```

```text
Events:  T1(10:00:02)  T2(10:00:05)  T3(10:00:03)  T4(10:00:08)
Max seen:    10:00:02      10:00:05      10:00:05      10:00:08
Watermark:   09:59:57      10:00:00      10:00:00      10:00:03
                                                        ▲
                                            Window [10:00:00, 10:00:01) fires
                                            because watermark > window end
```

### How do windows and watermarks work together?

The watermark drives window firing. A time-based window fires when the watermark passes the window end, meaning Flink believes all events for that window have arrived. Events that arrive after the watermark has passed their window are late; by default they are dropped.

```java
// Window fires when watermark > window_end
withWatermarks
    .keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    .sum("amount");
// Window [10:00, 10:01) fires when watermark reaches 10:01
// Late event at 10:00:30 arriving after watermark passed 10:01 → dropped
```

### What are the window components (assigner, trigger, evictor)?

Every window has three internal components. The assigner decides which window an element belongs to. The trigger decides when the window fires. The evictor optionally removes elements from the buffer.

```java
// Custom trigger: fire early every 50 elements AND on watermark
stream.keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    .trigger(CountTrigger.of(50)) // fire every 50 elements
    .evictor(CountEvictor.of(10)) // keep only last 10 elements
    .sum("amount");
```

### How do you handle late events?

You can keep windows open with allowed lateness or route late events to a side output.

```java
import org.apache.flink.util.OutputTag;

OutputTag<Transaction> lateTag = new OutputTag<>("late-txns") {};

SingleOutputStreamOperator<String> result = withWatermarks
    .keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    .allowedLateness(Time.minutes(5))  // keep window open 5 extra min
    .sideOutputLateData(lateTag)        // capture very late events
    .apply(windowFunction);

// Retrieve late events for reconciliation
DataStream<Transaction> lateEvents = result.getSideOutput(lateTag);
```

### What are common mistakes?

A common mistake is not setting a watermark, which means windows never fire because Flink has no assertion about time progress. Another mistake is using a very small out-of-orderness that drops too many slightly-delayed events.

```java
// WRONG -- no watermark, windows never fire
stream.keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    .sum("amount");  // Windows accumulate forever, never emit

// CORRECT -- watermark drives window firing
stream.assignTimestampsAndWatermarks(
        WatermarkStrategy.<Transaction>forBoundedOutOfOrderness(
            Duration.ofSeconds(5))
        .withTimestampAssigner((txn, ts) -> txn.getEventTimeMillis()))
    .keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    .sum("amount");  // Windows fire when watermark passes end
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The out-of-orderness tolerance trades latency against completeness. A larger value waits longer and catches more late events but adds delay, while a smaller value emits faster but drops more. For idle partitions, use `withIdleness(Duration)` so a stalled source does not hold back the global watermark and freeze all windows.
